    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A review of digital activity related to Jewish Studies in the United States since the\r\nearly 1990s would demonstrate that the intersection of information technology and\r\nJewish Studies is well established and evolving. The evolution of what is called Digital\r\nHumanities (DH) has not had such a clear or straight trajectory. Services and tools such\r\nas mailing-lists, academic portals, large digital libraries and archival digitization projects\r\nwere the dominant elements most Jewish Studies scholars thought of when imagining or\r\ndescribing digital Jewish Studies. These have been well documented in numerous articles,\r\nnewsletters, and the blogosphere. But scholars are now learning that as the numbers of\r\ndigital tools and media increase, and Jewish Studies faculty and researchers partner with\r\ninformation technologists, the possibilities for large research questions and discourse\r\nthat define the scholarly experience can grow exponentially.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
